__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.env
.DS_Store
.streamlit/secrets.toml
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# data/ is deliberately NOT ignored -- committing it is the whole mechanism by
# which snapshot-only sources ever acquire a history.
#
# THE ONE EXCEPTION, per source, is data we are not licensed to redistribute. The
# University of Michigan grants permission-free USE of its public Surveys of
# Consumers tables and separately prohibits redistribution without written consent
# (faq.php vs agreement.php -- see sources/umich.py for both quotes). Charting them
# is fine; mirroring them into a public repo is not. So this file is fetched and
# read like any other, and simply never committed.
#
# Do not add lines here by hand: a source declares `redistributable=False` and
# tests/test_sources.py asserts, via `git check-ignore`, that its parquet is
# ignored. Adding the flag without the line fails the suite, and vice versa.
data/umich_sca.parquet

# The SQLite store (lib/db). Deliberately NOT committed, for two reasons.
#
# It contains umich_sca, which is licensed for use and not redistribution -- the same
# rule as the line above, and tests/test_db.py asserts this one too.
#
# And it does not need committing: nine of the ten sources are backfillable, so losing
# this file costs one `scripts/ingest --backfill`. The tenth, openrouter_pricing,
# serves only "today" and its history is genuinely irreplaceable -- so it does NOT
# live here. It lives in git as one immutable CSV per snapshot date under
# data/snapshots/, which must stay committed.
data/*.sqlite
data/*.sqlite-wal
data/*.sqlite-shm

# Boards that were built and then set aside. Kept on disk so they are cheap to
# revive, out of the tree so the repo shows only what ships. They are still in
# git history: `git show 42aa2dd:panels/_parked/screen.py` recovers any of them.
.parked/

# Claude Code's working directory: ignore everything in it EXCEPT the settings we
# want shared.
#
# The pattern is `.claude/*` and NOT `.claude/`, and that is load-bearing rather
# than stylistic. Git will not re-include a file whose parent directory has been
# excluded by a directory pattern -- it never descends into an excluded directory to
# find the exception. So `.claude/` followed by `!.claude/settings.local.json`
# silently does nothing and the file stays ignored while the .gitignore reads as
# though it were tracked. Excluding the CONTENTS (`.claude/*`) leaves the directory
# itself visible, which is what makes the negation bite.
#
# What the blanket rule is really for is worktrees/: `git worktree add` puts linked
# checkouts there, each a full copy of the tree including data/, 118 MB at the time
# of writing. Each also contains a `.git` FILE, so git sees a nested repository and
# `git add -A` either warns and skips or writes a gitlink -- both awkward to undo.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.local.json
